{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ESCALATION (deferral gate) — a causal-stability design decision is required before any implementation\n\nIndependently re-investigated against the ticket AC, docs/concurrency.md §I1, epic b306 (adept-hedge-stain), the compaction code (src/rebar/_commands/compact.py, the RETIRED_SUFFIX/is_active_event contract in src/rebar/reducer/_cache.py), and the sibling escalation on degradable-appalling-cavy (32d4-9ff7-f582-453f). Conclusion matches the prior triage: this task cannot be implemented without an operator design decision, and the deferral gate applies. No code touched; ticket left open and unclaimed; no --force used.\n\n### The blocker is structural, not a matter of effort\nThe AC requires GC to remove `*.retired` files \"provably past causal stability\" and to honor I1–I9 (never remove a `.retired` whose companion `.json` could still be reintroduced by an in-flight rebase/merge). But:\n- docs/concurrency.md §I1 states retired files are kept **permanently for now** — \"an accepted storage tradeoff\" — and that a branch-wide GC sweep is \"safe only **past causal stability** (once no clone can still be mid-reconvergence against the pre-compaction events)\". That horizon is documented as a follow-up owned by THIS ticket; it is not built.\n- The tracker has **no registry of clones** and no observed-compaction watermark. In the current concurrency model there is therefore **no algorithm that can *prove* every clone has observed a compaction**. A long-offline clone that rebases pre-compaction events reintroduces the folded `.json`; if its `.retired` tombstone was already GC'd, that resurrects a folded source (violating the I1 guarantee that a folded source can never re-enter a SNAPSHOT_INCONSISTENT state).\n- The sibling ticket degradable-appalling-cavy escalated for the same reason, explicitly recording that \"no proof of causal stability currently exists\" and that merge-safe reclaim of retired sidecars is \"a subset of, and blocked on,\" the horizon owned here.\n\nAny horizon we pick is therefore a **heuristic**, and a wrong heuristic silently violates I1. Meeting the AC as written (\"provably\") is impossible in the current model without a doctrine change.\n\n### Decision needed — pick a stability model (each changes the AC and/or doctrine)\n1. **Time-based configurable horizon** (retain >= N days after the folding compaction commit): simplest, but PROBABILISTIC — cannot satisfy the \"provable\" AC; requires the operator to renegotiate the AC to an accepted risk window.\n2. **Remote-anchored horizon** (GC only tombstones whose compaction commit has been reachable from the sync remote's tickets ref for >= N days): stronger — any clone that pushes/pulls converges through the remote — but still unprovable for a clone that has never fetched. Needs an accepted assumption (\"no clone stays partitioned longer than the horizon\") written into the doctrine.\n3. **Explicit convergence/acknowledgment protocol** (clones record observed-compaction watermarks in the store; GC deletes only below the min watermark): the only path to a *real* proof. This is a **new event kind + I1–I9 doctrine change → an ADR and a designed epic**, not a task.\n4. **Keep permanent retention (b306 status quo)** and close this as accepted-tradeoff if branch storage growth is not yet a real cost.\n\nRecommend the operator choose between (2) with a renegotiated AC, (3) as a designed ADR/epic (which degradable-appalling-cavy would then depend on), or (4). Until then the safe state is the documented status quo: permanent retention. Handing back for that decision."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787562370322697001,"uuid":"6aaf88e0-8e35-4b20-8bef-31f955c31fa2"}